{"id":4408,"date":"2021-12-06T15:22:40","date_gmt":"2021-12-06T23:22:40","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=4408"},"modified":"2024-11-11T13:46:22","modified_gmt":"2024-11-11T21:46:22","slug":"3-2-battery-cde","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-2-battery-cde\/","title":{"rendered":"3.2: Battery"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b402f63\" name=\"6a2213b402f63\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before You Fly<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\" wp-image-11766 alignleft\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"106\" height=\"107\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>PRE-FLIGHT WARNING<\/strong><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\">Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong the longevity of your drone, avoid flying into walls or other objects. Prior to takeoff, please review lesson <em>0.2 \u201cBefore You Fly\u201d.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b40309c\" name=\"6a2213b40309c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Explain the importance of measuring battery level<\/li>\n<li>Describe how the battery level is reported by your CoDrone EDU<\/li>\n<li>Use the get_battery_percentage function to measure the drone&#8217;s battery level<\/li>\n<li>Store and print the drone&#8217;s battery level<\/li>\n<li>Use battery level to restrict flight movements<\/li>\n<li>Use the LED to indicate battery level<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Battery Indicator<\/li>\n<li>Printing the Battery Level<\/li>\n<li>Before and After<\/li>\n<li>Limiting Takeoff<\/li>\n<li>Adding the LED<\/li>\n<li>Add Conditionals to Your LED Program<\/li>\n<li>Adding elif Statements<\/li>\n<li>Adding an else Statement<\/li>\n<li>Final LED Code<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: Battery Calculator<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Battery Charge Reporter<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4030fe\" name=\"6a2213b4030fe\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Battery Indicator<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Think of all the electronics that you use and how you know that their battery is low or dead on each of them. If you are on a laptop or phone, you will most likely see a battery icon on the top or bottom of your screen. If you used Blockly to program your CoDrone EDU, you probably saw the battery level on the bottom left-hand corner of the screen.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><em>Although, how can you tell if your CoDrone EDU is running out of battery in Python?<\/em> There are three methods that you can try out: printing out the battery level, limiting when your drone can take off, and programming the LED as an indicator.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4031a2\" name=\"6a2213b4031a2\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Printing the Battery Level<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You used the <strong>print<\/strong> function in previous lessons, and you can use it to print your CoDrone EDU\u2019s battery level too! Call the function <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">get_battery()<\/code> and store its value in a variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">battery<\/code>. Finally, print the battery level. We added the words \u201cBattery Level:\u201d to our print statement to label it for you! If you want to do the same, make sure you add a comma in between all the print parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\n\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Now run that code again! <em>Did it change? Go up? Go down?<\/em> Do not be alarmed if you run the code a few times, and it seems to change a bit, especially after doing certain flight commands. The battery percentage is an approximation from reading the battery <strong>voltage.\u00a0<\/strong>Don&#8217;t worry too much about voltage for now, just be aware that the battery percentage may fluctuate a bit.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b40320c\" name=\"6a2213b40320c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Before and After<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You probably won&#8217;t notice a big difference in the percentage yet, so add some flying!<\/p>\n<p>Print the battery percentage before and after hovering for 10 seconds.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\ndrone = Drone()\n\ndrone.pair()\n\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\nprint(\"Before: \", battery)\n\ndrone.takeoff()\ndrone.hover(10)\ndrone.land()\n\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\nprint(\"After: \", battery)\n\ndrone.close()\n\n<\/pre>\n<p><em>What was the difference?<\/em> Explore with various flight commands to see which one consumes the most energy.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b40326a\" name=\"6a2213b40326a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Limiting Takeoff<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this exercise, you will write a program that will either take off or do nothing, depending on the battery level.<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, include the program that you wrote in the last step, which will print out your drone\u2019s battery level. Next, write a conditional so that if the battery level is greater than 50, it will take off, hover for 2 seconds, and land. Else, it will print \u201cNot enough power!\u201d and stay grounded.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\ndrone = Drone()\n\ndrone.pair()\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\n\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\n\n\nif battery &gt; 50:\n\n    drone.takeoff()\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.hover(2)\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.land()\n\n\nelse:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not enough power!\")\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try changing the battery level in the conditional to a different number. <em>At what percentage does your drone really not have enough power to take off, land, and hover?<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4032cf\" name=\"6a2213b4032cf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding the LED<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The LED on your CoDrone EDU already acts as a battery indicator. When your battery is low, it flashes red. In this program, you will be adding more colors to indicate different battery percentages.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">First, include the code from the last two steps that will import libraries, pair with CoDrone EDU, and print the battery percentage. You will also need to import the time library to include the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">time.sleep()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function in your program.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\nimport time\ndrone = Drone()\ndrone.pair()\n\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\n\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\n\ntime.sleep(1)\n\ndrone.close()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b403342\" name=\"6a2213b403342\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Add Conditionals to Your LED Program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You are going to use conditionals to check your battery\u2019s percentage. Depending on your battery level, your CoDrone EDU&#8217;s LED will turn a certain color. You can change them to whatever you want, but we recommend you make them easy to tell apart so you know when you need to charge your battery. This is what we&#8217;re going to use for this lesson:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">100-76: solid green LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">75-51: solid yellow LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">50-26: solid red LED<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">25-0: will not fly<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, write an if statement that will change your CoDrone EDU\u2019s LED to green when the battery level is above 75%. Include a delay, so flight movements after the LED change aren\u2019t skipped, and then add a <strong>takeoff<\/strong> command.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">if battery &gt; 75:\n\n    drone.set_drone_LED(0, 255, 0, 100)\u00a0 # green\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4033a4\" name=\"6a2213b4033a4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding elif Statements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Do the same for 51-75% battery and then 26-50% battery, but with a few changes:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instead of if statements, use else if statements.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Instead of the battery being greater than 75%, it should be greater than 50% and then 25%.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elif battery &gt; 50:\n\n    drone.set_drone_LED(255,255,0,100) # yellow\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\n\nelif battery &gt; 25:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_drone_LED(255,0,0, 100) # red\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4033fb\" name=\"6a2213b4033fb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding an Else Statement<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Add an else statement to indicate that if none of the above statements are true and your battery is less than 25%, your drone does not have enough power to fly!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">else:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0print(\"Not enough power!\")<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">All the conditionals above 25% have a <strong>takeoff<\/strong> command included, so include any flight commands you would like in your program after the else statement. <em>Don\u2019t forget to land and close your drone!<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b40344a\" name=\"6a2213b40344a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 11<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Final LED Code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your final code should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from codrone_edu.drone import *\n\nimport time\n\ndrone = Drone()\n\ndrone.pair()\n\n\nbattery = drone.get_battery()\n\nprint(\"Battery Level: \", battery)\n\ntime.sleep(1)\n\n\nif battery &gt; 75:\n\n    drone.set_drone_LED(0, 255, 0, 100)\u00a0 # green\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\n\nelif battery &gt; 50:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_drone_LED(255,255,0,100) # yellow\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\n\nelif battery &gt; 25:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.set_drone_LED(255,0,0, 100) # red\n\n\u00a0\u00a0 \u00a0time.sleep(0.05)\u00a0 # delay so takeoff doesn't skip\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 drone.takeoff()\n\nelse:\n\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0 print(\"Not enough power!\")\n\n\n# Insert your own flight pattern here\n\ndrone.set_pitch(100)\n\ndrone.move(1)\n\ndrone.land()\n\ndrone.close()\n<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b4034ad\" name=\"6a2213b4034ad\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 12<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 1: Battery Calculator<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we want to predict whether our drone will be able to fly the flight path we have programmed for it. To do this, repeat the step where we checked the difference in battery power after a takeoff, wait, and land. Figure out how much battery is lost per 5 seconds of flight time:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Takeoff, hover 5 seconds, land \u2192 take before and after battery measurements<\/li>\n<li>Takeoff, hover 10 seconds, land \u2192 take before and after battery measurements<\/li>\n<li>Take the difference of the battery usage between steps 1 and 2. This is your battery drain per 5 seconds of stationary flight.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Now, write a flight plan that has at least 5 movements and at least 30 seconds of flight time. Store the beginning and ending battery percentage. Also store the beginning and ending flight time. Print both of these values at the end of the flight, after landing.<\/p>\n<p>When you run the program, time it and make a prediction as to how much battery life you will lose. Check your drone&#8217;s output versus your prediction. <em>How close was your prediction? What could be causing any differences?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b403508\" name=\"6a2213b403508\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 13<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge 2: Battery Charge Reporter<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]For this challenge, we want to find out what the most battery intensive actions are so that we don&#8217;t tax our drone too much when flying. To complete this challenge, complete the following steps:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a list that contains the names of the maneuvers your drone will perform. (For example, flip, circle, square, hover).<\/li>\n<li>Create an empty list for battery levels.<\/li>\n<li>Call the functions in the list you created in step 1. At the end of each step, append to the list the current battery level and time.<\/li>\n<li>At the end of the program, print the list of maneuvers with their battery levels. You must use a loop to do this.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><strong>Bonus Challenge: <\/strong>If you dare, try to figure out the maneuver that uses the most battery and print this as well.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213b40354c\" name=\"6a2213b40354c\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 14<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In this lesson, you learned about the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s battery sensor and how it can be used to report the drone&#8217;s battery level. You also learned how to use that battery level value to restrict flight movements in your code.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7546&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 15 Before You Fly [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1730227373096{background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] PRE-FLIGHT WARNING Our drones are durable, yet not indestructible. Please exercise caution while piloting your drone. To prolong&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-2-battery-cde\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4735,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3875,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2154,4328,14401,2156,2122,2134,2155],"class_list":["post-4408","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-battery","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-intermediate","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-sensor","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4408","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":22,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4408\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13218,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/4408\/revisions\/13218"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4735"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=4408"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=4408"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=4408"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}